  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "reading-order",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 380,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The merchandiser's day, left to right: the morning workspace replaces the Excel order book; the order page carries the TNA and the size grid; the sampling room shows the PP gate; and MARBIM turns a buyer email into a draft the merchandiser verifies."
    },
    {
      "id": "dossier-note",
      "x": 3120,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 350,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The order's second tab, mirroring the merchandiser's real paper trail: tech pack and measurement spec, open costing revisions, the confirmation sheet signed through the chairman, sales contract, BTB budget ledger, fabric booking and mill PI, sample requisitions — each department's sign-off visible under the order."
    },
    {
      "id": "bangla-note",
      "x": 4680,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 340,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The same morning screen in Bangla — the app's en/bn toggle already carries it. Identifiers and money stay Latin, per the app's typography rules. Status now reads three ways at once: color, shape, word."
    },
    {
      "id": "marbim-drafts-only",
      "x": 3060,
      "y": 2420,
      "w": 300,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "MARBIM drafts; the merchandiser verifies. They correct values in place or ask for a specific change, and Verify applies the update as them — the audit trail keeps the draft, the corrections, and who verified."
    },
    {
      "id": "additions-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -240,
      "w": 420,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "Eight proposals, sketched from the factory's own documents rather than invented: the morning digest, the enquiry-to-quote lane with its 24-hour clock, the inputs readiness matrix (their In-House Check List, with cells that take a date OR a word OR a note), the chasing queue, a buyer-safe status pack, multi-drop POs, per-colour lab dip approvals, and keyboard shortcuts. Nothing here is decided — they are here to be argued with."
    },
    {
      "id": "papers-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -260,
      "w": 430,
      "page": "page-4",
      "text": "Seven more, each answering something the factory's own documents already do and the app does not: order memory with repeat-order drift, the fabric's journey from mill to store with the booking's penalty ladder, ship-date history as a trail rather than an overwritten field, a pre-filled sample requisition, a breakdown grid that survives leg lengths and ratio packs, capacity and the two price floors, and the buying agent as a real party."
    },
    {
      "id": "plumbing-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -260,
      "w": 430,
      "page": "page-5",
      "text": "Five that serve people around the merchandiser, or the plumbing under them: the owner's read-only view of the same book, an alert model with only three channels and a rule for what never becomes an alert, a buyer scorecard honest about how thin its data still is, mail intake for the inbox where work really arrives, and a print sheet, because they print for meetings."
    },
    {
      "id": "tna-options",
      "x": 1560,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 340,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "Three ways to show Time and action, same data. Option A is the table on the order page — the Excel habit, dense and printable. Option B answers 'where are we against the calendar' in one glance. Option C peeks at any order's TNA without leaving the book. They can coexist: table on the page, drawer from the book, timeline as a toggle."
    }
  ],
